body:
An intersection reconciled keys by scanning a key list once per key, both for the keys the two sides output (the value merge) and for the keys both sides rejected (strict objects and records that reject unknown keys). Its cost grew with the square of the input's key count, on valid input as well as invalid.

```ts
const input = Object.fromEntries(Array.from({ length: 40_000 }, (_, i) => [`k${i}`, i]));
z.intersection(z.record(z.string(), z.number()), z.record(z.string(), z.number())).parse(input);
// ~0.9 s before, ~60 ms after; one record alone is ~12 ms
z.intersection(z.strictObject({}), z.strictObject({})).safeParse(input);
// ~1.7 s before, ~16 ms after
```

Both now take one lookup per key. The shared-key test is `propertyIsEnumerable`, which is exactly membership in `Object.keys()`, so the merged value is unchanged, and the reported issues come out in the same order. The compiled path calls the same merge. Those timings were taken on a heavily loaded machine; the growth is the point (~4x per doubling before, ~2x after).

| axis | result |
| --- | --- |
| runtime | quadratic to linear in key count. Small intersections are no slower (interleaved minimums, taken at load 300+, so indicative only): 223 → 196 ns for a shared-key merge, 226 → 212 disjoint, 855 → 732 for a strict-object failure, 10-key records 2,403 → 2,357 |
| memory | per-schema retained bytes unchanged (`z.intersection(obj, obj)` 5466 → 5466). A parse no longer allocates the key arrays it used to scan |
| bundle | classic intersection fixture −19 B gzipped (24,754 → 24,735); `zod/mini` intersection −12 B (5,057 → 5,045); fixtures without an intersection unchanged |

A tuple's rest loop shared one `let i` across iterations, so every async rest element resolved into the last index and left the ones before it empty:

```ts
z.tuple([z.string()], z.string().refine(async () => true)).parseAsync(["a", "b", "c", "d"]);
// ["a", undefined, undefined, "d"], now ["a", "b", "c", "d"]
```

Issue paths had the same problem: a failing async rest element was reported at the last index. The loop now declares its index per iteration and reads `input[i]` directly, so it also stops copying the rest with `slice()`. Sync parses produced the right value before and run the same work minus that copy; bundle and per-schema memory are unchanged to within a few bytes.

## Summary

Adds a public, type-safe way to look up `discriminatedUnion` member schemas by their discriminator value. Refs #5086 (point: *"Access individual types via discriminant value"*).

Today the only way to do this is via the internal `_zod.propValues` map plus a manual `find` over `options`, both of which require type assertions and reach into undocumented surfaces.

## Changes

- **core**: promote the internal `discriminator → member` map to `_zod.optionsMap` (lazy), typed as `ReadonlyMap<Primitive, Options[number]>`. The parse path and the existing `invalid_union` issue keep working unchanged — they now read from the same `_zod.optionsMap`. No runtime behavior changes.
- **classic**: on `ZodDiscriminatedUnion`, add:
  - `optionsMap` — read-only map of all valid discriminator values to their option schemas.
  - `get(value)` — returns the precise member schema matching the literal value passed in, narrowed at the type level.

The type helpers (`$DiscriminatorValue`, `$DiscriminatedOption`) handle the tricky case where a single member declares **multiple** discriminator values via `z.literal([...])`, by inverting the inference (`V extends OV` rather than `OV extends V`).

## Example

```ts
const fruit = z.object({ type: z.literal("fruit"), seeds: z.boolean() });
const veg   = z.object({ type: z.literal("vegetable"), leafy: z.boolean() });
const schema = z.discriminatedUnion("type", [fruit, veg]);

const f = schema.get("fruit");        // typed as `typeof fruit`
schema.get("unknown");                // ts-error: not a valid discriminator value

for (const [v, member] of schema.optionsMap) {
  // ...
}
```

This also unblocks the type-safe error-discrimination workaround discussed in #5086 in user-land:

```ts
const disc = (input as any)?.[schema.def.discriminator];
const member = schema.get(disc);
if (member) {
  const r = member.safeParse(input);
  if (!r.success) return { type: disc, errors: z.treeifyError(r.error) };
}
```

